<section class="section section-dark" aria-labelledby="safety-title">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
      <h2 id="safety-title">A little upkeep goes a long way</h2>
      <p>
        Chimneys, stoves and vents do their jobs quietly. Most problems build up
        slowly, out of sight, and they're simple to catch with regular care.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="safety-grid">
      <article class="safety-item">
        <h3>Creosote buildup</h3>
        <p>Burning wood leaves creosote, a flammable tar-like residue, on the inside of the flue. Cleaning removes it before it builds into a hazard.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="safety-item">
        <h3>Chimney fire risk</h3>
        <p>Cracked liners, worn mortar and blockages can let heat reach combustible parts of the house. Inspections catch them while they're still easy to fix.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="safety-item">
        <h3>Carbon monoxide</h3>
        <p>Fuel-burning appliances make carbon monoxide, an odorless gas. A blocked chimney or vent can push it back indoors, so keep venting clear and CO alarms working.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="safety-item">
        <h3>Dryer vent lint</h3>
        <p>According to the U.S. Fire Administration, failure to clean is the leading factor in home clothes dryer fires.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="safety-note">
      The National Fire Protection Association standard NFPA 211 calls for chimneys,
      fireplaces and vents to be inspected once a year and cleaned as needed.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="process-title">
  <div class="container">
